# Cloudflare Pages configuration for redirects
# NOTE: the "!" forced-redirect suffix (301!) is NOT honoured on this
# deployment — verified in production: /home and /blog/post/:slug returned 404
# while every plain "301" rule resolved correctly. Plain 301 is safe here
# because no static file exists at these paths.
#
# NOTE (trailing-slash normalization, audit §1 T3): `/about` -> `/about/` is a
# 307 issued by Cloudflare's BUILT-IN asset serving (assets.html_handling,
# default "auto-trailing-slash"), not by these rules. The 307 status is NOT
# configurable — the html_handling docs' tables show 307 in every mode
# (auto/force/drop), the setting only picks which URL is canonical, and
# "none" would fall through to a 404 instead — and worker.ts never sees
# directory paths because the asset match short-circuits the Worker
# (run_worker_first = false). Exact-path 308 rules listed HERE would win (docs:
# "Redirects are always followed, regardless of whether or not an asset
# matches the incoming request"), but a generic "path lacks trailing slash"
# rule is not expressible (no negative/conditional matching in _redirects), so
# every slash-less variant of every page — including blog slugs the content
# team adds later — must be enumerated by hand, and none of it could be
# verified before deploy. Outcome: documented as host behaviour; the 307 still
# lands on the canonical trailing-slash URL, and absolute canonicals already
# normalize for search engines.
# /home -> / (redirect old React Router routes)
/home / 301

# Sitemap backward-compatibility redirect (Next.js -> Astro)
/sitemap.xml /sitemap-index.xml 301

# Legacy service worker cleanup redirect
/sw.js/ /sw.js 301

# Single-hop for the renamed "-2024" posts: without these, the generic
# /blog/post/:slug placeholder below rewrites to /blog/<slug>-2024/, which then
# needs a SECOND 301 to the evergreen URL (2-hop chain, audit §1 T5). Static
# rules sit above the dynamic placeholder because the top-most matching rule
# wins and static rules are evaluated before dynamic ones.
/blog/post/seo-guide-2024 /blog/seo-guide/ 301
/blog/post/seo-guide-2024/ /blog/seo-guide/ 301
/blog/post/google-ads-guide-2024 /blog/google-ads-guide/ 301
/blog/post/google-ads-guide-2024/ /blog/google-ads-guide/ 301
/blog/post/digital-marketing-trends-2024 /blog/digital-marketing-trends/ 301
/blog/post/digital-marketing-trends-2024/ /blog/digital-marketing-trends/ 301

# Blog post redirects for old React Router structure
/blog/post/:slug /blog/:slug/ 301

# Evergreen blog slugs: the "-2024" suffix aged badly in the SERP now that the
# content is genuinely current (1405 refresh). These three URLs are already
# live and indexed, so they MUST keep resolving. Both slash variants are listed
# because the legacy Next.js build served them with a trailing slash.
/blog/seo-guide-2024 /blog/seo-guide/ 301
/blog/seo-guide-2024/ /blog/seo-guide/ 301
/blog/google-ads-guide-2024 /blog/google-ads-guide/ 301
/blog/google-ads-guide-2024/ /blog/google-ads-guide/ 301
/blog/digital-marketing-trends-2024 /blog/digital-marketing-trends/ 301
/blog/digital-marketing-trends-2024/ /blog/digital-marketing-trends/ 301

